<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poona Pact</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a> Pact was an agreement of 24 September 1932 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> on the political representation of the depressed classes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, now known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a>. It answered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communal-award">Communal Award</a> of August 1932, which had granted the depressed classes separate electorates; Gandhi, imprisoned in Yerwada Central Jail in Poona, began a fast unto death against it, seeing it as a British attempt to divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. The pact, signed by 23 people, replaced separate electorates with 148 seats reserved for the depressed classes within joint electorates, nearly twice what the Award had offered. Its reserved seats endure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s legislatures.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>B. R. Ambedkar (1891–1956), the foremost leader of the untouchable castes, clashed with Gandhi in 1932, when Gandhi fasted against separate electorates for the depressed classes until Ambedkar accepted reserved seats instead in the <b>Poona Pact</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>B. R. Ambedkar, born into a caste classified as untouchable, became a leader of human-rights campaigns; at his request the Communal Award of 1932 gave the depressed classes separate electorates, which he gave up in the <b>Poona Pact</b> after Gandhi’s hunger strike.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>Agreed with Gandhi during his fast, the <b>Poona Pact</b> was signed, replacing separate electorates for the 'depressed classes' with reserved seats.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
